 ## Overview
-Gaussian Geometry Parameters Prediction refers to feed-forward methods that directly predict the geometric attributes (position, covariance, opacity) of 3D Gaussian primitives from input images or point clouds, bypassing the iterative per-scene optimization required by standard 3D Gaussian Splatting [S1, S2]. Unlike sibling methods that rely on heuristic densification or local cost volumes, this approach typically utilizes a global volumetric representation or learned priors to ensure multi-view consistency and reduce ghosting artifacts in overlapping regions [S2]. It enables real-time novel view synthesis and 3D reconstruction from sparse or uncalibrated inputs by mapping input data to a complete Gaussian set in a single pass [S1, S2].
+Gaussian Geometry Parameters Prediction is a feed-forward method that predicts the geometric attributes (position, opacity, and covariance) of 3D Gaussians from a global volumetric feature representation, rather than optimizing them iteratively per scene [S2]. It solves the problem of slow convergence and ghosting artifacts inherent in per-pixel aligned feed-forward methods by accumulating depth information into a unified 3D volume [S2]. Unlike its siblings which often rely on local cost volumes or epipolar transformers, this approach predicts a global scene representation, improving consistency in urban-scale reconstruction [S2].
 
